     <p>Workbench Preview for Chrome saves named web destinations and returns to them in their paired Chrome profiles. It requires the matching <a href="https://workbench-mac.vercel.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Workbench Preview companion for macOS</a>. Ethan Harley publishes this open-source extension.</p>
 
     <h2>What the extension handles</h2>
-    <p>The extension handles the destination names and Chrome profile labels you enter, a generated profile identifier, the addresses you choose to save, and temporary tab/window identifiers used to focus a destination. Opening the popup reads the current tab’s address locally so you can review it. Query parameters and fragments are removed before an address is saved or sent to the companion. Usernames and passwords embedded in an address are rejected.</p>
+    <p>The extension handles the destination names and Chrome profile labels you enter, a generated profile identifier, the addresses you choose to save, and temporary tab/window identifiers used to focus a destination. Opening the popup reads the current tab’s address locally so you can review it. Query parameters and fragments are removed before a destination address is saved or sent to the companion. Usernames and passwords embedded in an address are rejected.</p>
     <p>To find a saved destination, the extension checks permitted tabs’ current or pending addresses. It does not read page content, forms, passwords, cookies or browsing history. It does not sign in, rotate passwords or confirm which account is authenticated. Page titles are not used as destination names.</p>
 
     <h2>Where this data goes</h2>
     <p>Chrome stores the profile label, generated identifier and pairing state locally in that profile. Tab bindings and an unfinished save draft stay in Chrome’s session storage. A permission-step draft can be restored for 15 minutes; an expired draft is not used and may remain in session storage until overwritten or the browser session ends.</p>
     <p>The extension sends chosen names, saved addresses and profile labels to the local Workbench companion through Chrome’s native-messaging connection. The companion owns the canonical Saved resources records on your Mac and returns the destination list to your connected Chrome profiles. Browser destinations are not part of Workbench’s optional photo or scene sync.</p>
+    <p>After pairing with a compatible companion, the extension also reports whether this Chrome profile has a focused window and the observation time. The companion can use this state to associate its menu and presentation tools with the active profile. These events contain no tab identifiers, addresses, page titles, content or browsing history.</p>
     <p>This feature has no developer-operated data server, analytics, advertising, sale of data or transmission of destination records to Ethan Harley or other third parties. Chrome Sync is not used for these records. When you open a destination, Chrome contacts that website normally; the website’s own policies apply. Visiting the companion website or support links also makes a normal web request to that provider.</p>
     <p>Workbench’s use of data received through Chrome APIs complies with the Chrome Web Store User Data Policy, including its Limited Use requirements.</p>
 
+    <h2>Optional Chrome bookmarks</h2>
+    <p>Bookmarks are accessed only after you choose <strong>Allow bookmarks</strong> in this profile. The extension reads recent bookmarks or matches a search and returns at most 30 results. It uses read-only bookmark methods: it does not add, change, delete, import or copy your bookmark library. Chrome remains the source of truth, and Chrome’s own bookmark-sync settings still apply.</p>
+    <p>The popup and compatible local Workbench menu can receive each match’s bookmark identifier, cleaned label and hostname. Full bookmark addresses, including query parameters and fragments, stay inside Chrome. When you select a bookmark, its exact identifier is resolved again in the same profile and the complete original web address opens in a new tab. This does not need permission to inspect that website. Non-web addresses and embedded credentials are rejected. Bookmark results and search queries are not stored by the extension.</p>
+    <p>Bookmark labels can still describe private work. Review your labels and sharing setup; opening a tab does not verify authentication, page readiness or what a meeting audience can see.</p>
+
     <h2>Why Chrome asks for permissions</h2>
     <ul>
       <li><strong>Communicate with cooperating native applications:</strong> connects only to the Workbench native host so Saved resources can route a destination to its paired profile.</li>
